course: everyday
lesson: 86
title: Sun, cold and growing up
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Strong sun burns skin, and too much heat or cold upsets the body: heatstroke and hypothermia are emergencies, so we protect ourselves before they start.
explain: In Australia and New Zealand, where the sun is very strong, children learn «slip, slop, slap, seek, slide»: a shirt, sunscreen, a hat, shade and sunglasses.
explain: Between about 8 and 14, the body starts to change from a child's body to an adult's; this is called puberty, it is caused by hormones, and it happens at a different time for everyone.
explain: A girl in Phoenix plays outside in the morning, stays in the cool at midday during a heatwave, keeps a water bottle with her, and wears a hat and sunscreen.
fact: Use broad-spectrum sunscreen of SPF 30 or more (the bigger the SPF number, the stronger the protection) and put it on 20 minutes before going out, because sunburn damages skin and raises the risk of skin cancer later.
fact: «Slip, slop, slap, seek, slide» means slip on a shirt, slop on sunscreen, slap on a hat, seek shade and slide on sunglasses.
fact: If you get sunburnt, get out of the sun, cool the skin with a cool shower, drink water and tell an adult.
fact: Someone in the heat who is confused, very hot and has stopped sweating may have heatstroke, which is an emergency: call the emergency number.
fact: In a heatwave, drink often, wear light clothes and stay cool indoors at the hottest time of day.
fact: Warm frozen fingers gently with body heat or lukewarm water, never on a radiator or fire, because numb skin burns easily.
fact: Shivering a lot and feeling slow after playing in the cold can be the start of hypothermia: go inside, take off wet clothes and get warm.
fact: Never lick a frozen metal pole, because your tongue sticks to it and the skin tears.
fact: Puberty happens at different times for everyone, usually starting between about 8 and 14.
fact: During puberty, sweat glands become more active, so wash every day and use deodorant.
fact: Wash spots gently twice a day and do not squeeze them, because squeezing can cause scars and infection.
fact: Everyone grows at their own pace, and a boy's voice cracks for a while as his voice box grows.
fact: If you have questions about your body, ask a parent, teacher or doctor, and learn the proper names of body parts so you can talk clearly.
exercise: How should you use sunscreen on a sunny day? | SPF 30 or more, 20 minutes before going out | SPF 5, put on only after you are already burnt | Any cream, once at the end of the day | None if you already have a tan
exercise: What does «slap» mean in «slip, slop, slap, seek, slide»? | Put on a hat | Put on sunscreen | Look for shade | Put on sunglasses
exercise: You got sunburnt at the park. What should you do? | Get out of the sun, cool off, drink water and tell an adult | Put on more sunscreen and stay out in the sun for the rest of the day | Rub butter on the red skin | Sit in the sun to toughen the skin
exercise: On a hot day, a man is confused, very hot and has stopped sweating. What do you do? | Call the emergency number | Give him a hot drink | Tell him to go for a jog | Wait for him to feel better
exercise: What should you do in a heatwave? | Drink often and stay cool indoors at the hottest time | Run races at midday so your body gets used to the heat | Wear thick dark clothes against the sun | Drink only once a day so you sweat less
exercise: Your fingers are numb with cold after building a snowman. How do you warm them? | Gently, with body heat or lukewarm water | Holding them flat against a hot radiator | Putting them close to the fire | Running them under very hot water from the tap
exercise: After sledging you are wet, shivering a lot and slow. What should you do? | Go inside, take off wet clothes and get warm | Stay out longer so your body learns to get used to it | Eat snow to cool down | Run around in your wet clothes
exercise: Why should you never lick a frozen metal pole? | Your tongue sticks and the skin tears | The pole is full of germs | It makes the pole go rusty | Your tongue turns blue for a week or more
exercise: What is puberty? | The changes from a child's body to an adult's | An illness that children catch from each other at school | A rule about growing up at 13 | The time when children stop growing
exercise: You have started to smell sweaty under your arms. What should you do? | Wash every day and use deodorant | Never take off your jumper | Stop doing any sport | Put on more perfume instead of washing
exercise: You have spots on your face. What is best? | Wash gently twice a day and do not squeeze | Squeeze them as soon as they appear on your face | Scrub them hard with soap | Cover them with thick make-up
exercise: You are much shorter than your friends. What is true? | Everyone grows at their own pace | Something must be wrong with you | You will always stay short | Eating more sweets makes you taller
exercise: A boy's voice keeps cracking. Why? | His voice box is growing during puberty | He has caught a bad throat infection | He has been shouting too much | He is losing his voice for good
exercise: Why learn the proper names of body parts? | It helps you talk clearly to doctors and adults | It is something that only doctors need to know | Proper names are rude | Children should not know them
test: A boy in Melbourne is going to the beach. Which plan follows the sun rule? | Shirt, sunscreen, hat, shade and sunglasses | Sunscreen put on only once, early in the morning | A hat only when it is cloudy | No shirt, so he tans evenly
test: Why does sunburn matter even after it stops hurting? | It damages skin and raises the risk of skin cancer later | It makes your hair grow faster | It means you can never go out in the sun again for the rest of the summer | It turns into freckles that hurt
test: A girl in Minnesota comes in from the snow with icy hands. Her brother says to hold them by the fire. What is right? | Warm them gently, as numb skin burns easily | Hold them by the fire, it is fastest | Put them straight into a bowl of very hot water | Rub them with snow first
test: Why is heatstroke an emergency? | The body cannot cool itself any more | The person is only very thirsty and needs a drink | It just means a bad sunburn | It means the person needs a nap
test: A 9-year-old girl notices her body starting to change before her friends'. What is true? | Puberty starts at different times for everyone | She is ill and must see a doctor about it at once | Puberty always starts at exactly 12 | Only boys go through puberty
test: Who is a good person to ask about questions on growing up? | A parent, teacher or doctor | A stranger you meet online | Only other children | No one, it is a secret
test: Why can wet clothes in the cold be dangerous? | They make the body lose heat fast | They make the body too hot | They are heavy and slow you down | They stop you from shivering
test: A boy squeezes a spot on his nose. What may happen? | It can cause a scar or infection | It makes the spot disappear for ever | It stops new spots from growing | Nothing at all can happen
test: put sunscreen on as we walk out the door | Put it on 20 minutes before going out | Right as you walk out of the door is best | After an hour in the sun | Only once you feel hot
test: so cold my fingers r numb, put em on the radiator | No, warm them gently, numb skin burns easily | Yes, hot radiators warm fastest | Yes, or hold them near the fire to thaw them | Yes, but only for a minute
test: mate dared me lick the frozen pole lol | No, your tongue sticks and the skin tears | Yes, it tastes just like a cold ice lolly | Yes, just a quick lick | Yes, if you blow on it first
test: my mate's already got a deeper voice n im 11 n nothing, whats wrong w me | Nothing, puberty starts at different times | You are ill and need medicine from the doctor | You will never grow up | Your voice will never change
explain: A boy in Queensland spent a beach day without sunscreen or a shirt; his back blistered with sunburn, he had a fever and could not sleep for nights.
explain: A girl in Alaska fell through thin ice on a pond while walking her dog; she was pulled out by an adult quickly, but she was so cold she had to be warmed in hospital.
fact: Bad sunburns in childhood raise the chance of skin cancer later in life.
fact: A tan is a sign the sun has already harmed the skin, not a shield, so tanned skin still needs sunscreen.
fact: Ice on ponds and lakes can look solid but be too thin to hold a person.
fact: If a person or a pet falls through ice, you never go onto the ice after them; you shout for an adult and call the emergency number.
test: beach all day, sunscreen is for babies lol | No, sunburn blisters and raises skin cancer risk, slip slop slap | Yes, children don't burn | Yes, clouds block all the sun | Only wear it if it's over 40 degrees
test: pond's frozen, can we walk on it | No, ice can be too thin and break, stay off | Yes, if it looks white it's safe | Yes, if you run fast across | Only near the middle
test: dog ran onto the ice and fell in | Don't go after it, shout for an adult and call the emergency number | Run onto the ice to grab it | Jump in to help it swim | Throw stones to break a path
test: my cousin in Queensland says she never wears sunscreen cos she "doesn't burn", she's got a tan already | Sun still damages tanned skin, so slip, slop, slap, seek, slide anyway | A tan blocks all sun damage, so she is right | Only people with pale skin can get skin cancer | Sunscreen is only needed at the beach in summer
test: heard a kid in Minnesota licked a frozen flagpole n his tongue came OFF, like fully off | The tongue can stick and the skin tear, so never lick frozen metal; fully off is exaggerated | It is true, frozen metal always pulls whole tongues off | It is a made-up story, frozen metal is safe to lick | It only happens with flagpoles, not other frozen metal
test: everyone's laughing that Tom's voice keeps cracking in class, they're doing impressions, should i | No, voices crack as they grow in puberty; do not join in the teasing | Yes, it is only a joke, he will not mind | Yes, but only when the teacher is not looking | Yes, because his voice is cracking on purpose
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the recommended use of sunscreen? | A broad-spectrum sunscreen of SPF 30 or more is applied about twenty minutes before going out | A sunscreen of any factor is applied once a summer, as protection persists for several months | A sunscreen is required solely on cloudy days, since sunshine itself does not burn the skin | A sunscreen is applied only after sunburn has developed, in order to soothe the damaged skin
exam: A person exposed to great heat is observed to be confused, very hot and no longer sweating. Identify the appropriate response. | It may be heatstroke, an emergency, and the emergency number is telephoned | It is ordinary tiredness, and the person is advised to continue with their activity | It is a mild sunburn, and a cream is applied before the person returns to the sun | It is a sign of good health, as the body has ceased to waste water through sweat
exam: Determine the correct method for the rewarming of fingers which have become frozen and numb. | Gentle warming with body heat or lukewarm water, as numb skin burns readily | Direct placement upon a hot radiator, since rapid heating is the most effective | Holding the fingers close to an open fire until sensation has fully returned | Vigorous rubbing with snow in order that the circulation may be restored
exam: In respect of the age at which puberty commences, select the response consistent with the established facts. | It begins at different times for each person, usually between about eight and fourteen | It begins for every child on precisely the same birthday, namely at the age of twelve | It begins only after the age of sixteen, and never during the years of primary school | It begins at the same time for all members of a single class, irrespective of age
